Usage:
  nucleus migrate [flags] [<action> [args]]

Apply and manage SQL migrations against the configured database. The action is required: a bare nucleus migrate is a usage error, never an implicit up.

Actions:
  up [n]         Apply every pending migration, or only the next n
  down [n]       Roll back the last migration, or the last n (destructive: --force or --yes in production)
  steps <n>      Apply n migrations forward, or roll back |n| when n is negative
  status         List every migration file with its applied/pending state
  drift          Report applied migrations whose .up.sql file is missing on disk; exits non-zero on drift
  reset          Roll back every applied migration (destructive)
  refresh        Roll back every applied migration, then apply all of them again (destructive)
  create <name>  Write a <timestamp>_<name>.up.sql/.down.sql pair: empty, or rendered from your Quark models with --from-models <dir>; needs no database

Flags:
  -config string
    	Path to nucleus config file
  -database string
    	Database alias to use (defaults to database_default)
  -dialect string
    	create --from-models: SQL dialect (sqlite|postgresql|mysql|mssql|oracle); defaults to the configured database's
  -force
    	Force destructive actions (recommended in CI)
  -from-models string
    	create: render the pair from the Quark models in this directory through the quark CLI (makemigrations passes internal/models)
  -migrations string
    	Migrations directory (default "migrations")
  -yes
    	Auto-confirm destructive actions without prompt

Examples:
  nucleus migrate --config nucleus.yml up
  nucleus migrate --config nucleus.yml status
  nucleus migrate --config nucleus.yml down 1
  nucleus migrate create add_users_table
  nucleus migrate create init_schema --from-models internal/models

nucleus makemigrations <name> is create --from-models internal/models when the project has that package (where the generators write Quark models), else create <name>. create --from-models runs the quark CLI (`quark` on PATH, or go run github.com/jcsvwinston/quark/cmd/quark@latest; NUCLEUS_QUARK_CLI pins one) with --emit json and writes the DDL it prints, for the configured database's dialect or --dialect. It needs cmd/quark v1.3.0 or later. A models directory without a Quark model gets the empty pair.

Each migration runs in one transaction with the ledger row that records it. That makes it all-or-nothing only where DDL is transactional: PostgreSQL, SQLite and SQL Server. MySQL and MariaDB commit at every DDL statement and Oracle DDL commits itself, so there a migration that fails part-way leaves what ran before the failure applied and no ledger row — undo it by hand before running up again. Keeping one DDL statement per migration on those engines limits what a failure can leave behind.
